  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 148 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nGlobal model guidance is coming into better agreement on the \nejection of an upper-level trough into the Central/Northern Plains \nand Upper Midwest Monday through Tuesday as the Omega Block breaks \ndown. With this track of the trough, it is increasingly likely that \nthe associated cold front does not reach the CWA, favoring similar, \nseasonably warm temperatures continuing along with dry conditions.\n\nIt is not until late next week now, that there are any signs for our \nsensible weather to change with varying signals for an upper-level \ntrough to propagate into the north-central CONUS. However, the track \nand amplitude of the trough is uncertain which will influence its \nability to force an associated cold front east/southeastward. If the \nfront reaches the CWA, it would be accompanied by an opportunity for \nrain and temperatures cooling closer to average late next week. If \nthe front fails to reach the CWA, there would be no clear catalyst \nfor seasonably warm temperatures and dry conditions to end. Ensemble \nmodel guidance cluster analysis depicts these scenarios all being \nvalid possible outcomes at this point. Taking all scenarios into \nconsideration, probabilities of measurable rainfall range from 20 to \n50 percent Thursday, onward, along with NBM interquartile ranges \nincreasing to between 10 and 20 F.",
